## 8. Security Best Practices

### Always Validate Input
```php
// ✅ CORRECT - Use Form Requests
namespace App\Http\Requests;

class StoreOrderRequest extends FormRequest
{
    public function authorize(): bool
    {
        return $this->user()->can('create', Order::class);
    }
    
    public function rules(): array
    {
        return [
            'number' => ['required', 'string', 'max:255', 'unique:orders'],
            'client_id' => ['required', 'exists:clients,id'],
            'price_to_customer' => ['nullable', 'numeric', 'min:0', 'max:999999.99'],
        ];
    }
}
```

### Use Policies for Authorization
```php
// ✅ CORRECT - Define policies
namespace App\Policies;

class OrderPolicy
{
    public function update(User $user, Order $order): bool
    {
        // Only order owner or admin can update
        return $user->hasPermissionTo('orders.edit')
            && ($order->user_id === $user->id || $user->hasRole('admin'));
    }
    
    public function finish(User $user, Order $order): bool
    {
        // Only admin can finish orders
        return $user->hasPermissionTo('orders.finish')
            && $user->hasRole('admin');
    }
}

// Use in Filament resource
public static function canEdit(Model $record): bool
{
    return auth()->user()->can('update', $record);
}
```

### Prevent Mass Assignment Vulnerabilities
```php
// ✅ CORRECT - Use $fillable (whitelist)
class Order extends Model
{
    protected $fillable = [
        'number',
        'description',
        'client_id',
        // Only specify fields that should be mass-assignable
    ];
}

// ❌ WRONG - Using $guarded with ['id'] allows too much
protected $guarded = ['id']; // Dangerous!
```

